Eh, it's not
the same.

13:16.287 --> 13:19.355
Anyway, he and Brian have been
spending a lot of time together.

13:19.390 --> 13:21.357
Really? You're going
to let that slide?

13:21.392 --> 13:23.059
Guy throws
a biscuit at you?

13:23.093 --> 13:24.260
Good evening, all.

13:24.295 --> 13:25.995
Apologies for being so tardy.

13:26.030 --> 13:27.330
Aw, Chris,
you're not tardy.

13:27.364 --> 13:28.865
We tested you twice.

13:28.899 --> 13:31.868
There you guys are.
We were wondering about you.

13:31.902 --> 13:33.736
Brian and I have been
having a blast.

13:33.771 --> 13:35.338
First we went
to a symphony.

13:35.372 --> 13:37.106
Then we saw a small
independent play

13:37.141 --> 13:38.908
with real smoking in it.

13:38.943 --> 13:40.810
And then we hit
the Quahog Book Festival

13:40.844 --> 13:42.078
and went to a Q and A

13:42.112 --> 13:44.480
to ask questions that
weren't really questions.

13:44.515 --> 13:47.517
Wow, look at my sophisticated
young man.

13:47.551 --> 13:50.086
You're certainly taking
to Brian's example.

13:50.120 --> 13:51.955
If anything,
he's teaching me.

13:51.989 --> 13:53.923
I mean, not really,
but you-you know.

13:53.958 --> 13:55.391
Aw, see, I knew Brian

13:55.426 --> 13:57.226
could put Chris on
the path to success.

13:57.261 --> 13:58.628
Hey, Chris,
let's celebrate.

13:58.662 --> 14:00.430
There's a new baby
panda at the zoo.

14:00.464 --> 14:02.765
How about you and me
go chuck tater tots at it?

14:02.800 --> 14:03.766
Chuck them at Lois.

14:03.801 --> 14:04.901
She'll just sit there
and take it.

14:04.935 --> 14:05.969
Oh, Dad.

14:06.003 --> 14:07.870
That would've been fine
for the old me,

14:07.905 --> 14:09.939
but Brian and I
have to be going.

14:09.974 --> 14:12.575
Oh. Okay.

14:12.609 --> 14:14.043
Yeah, we're off to see
an old movie

14:14.078 --> 14:16.612
on temporary screen
in a muddy, freezing park.

14:16.647 --> 14:18.081
That sounds awful.

14:18.115 --> 14:19.349
It is awful.

14:19.383 --> 14:21.617
But at least parking
will be impossible.

14:21.652 --> 14:23.987
Bye!

14:24.021 --> 14:28.891
Geez, I... feel like
I just... lost my son.

14:28.926 --> 14:30.960
Aw, I got a skin tag in my crack

14:30.995 --> 14:32.996
and it stings
when I don't wipe right.

14:33.030 --> 14:34.664
This has been
a bad dinner.

14:40.805 --> 14:42.038
There you two are.

14:42.072 --> 14:43.206
Sit down and join us.

14:43.240 --> 14:44.374
I can't stay, Mom.

14:44.408 --> 14:45.408
Brian's showing me

14:45.443 --> 14:46.976
all of his favorite
stuff to do.

14:47.011 --> 14:48.645
Getting spooked when
the furnace goes on?

14:48.679 --> 14:50.880
We're participating
in an autism walk.

14:50.915 --> 14:52.649
Looks like you're
doing it right now.

14:52.683 --> 14:54.117
And we may be gone a while,

14:54.151 --> 14:55.919
'cause after that we're going
down to the Civic Center.

14:55.953 --> 14:58.755
Michael Ian Black is cocking
his eyebrows for four hours.

14:58.789 --> 15:02.058
Ha! He says the things
we don't care to say.

15:02.092 --> 15:03.493
Bye!

15:04.862 --> 15:07.630
Man, I'm sick of how much time
Chris is spending with Brian.

15:07.665 --> 15:09.065
Probably smooching
pickles,

15:09.099 --> 15:10.667
'cause they're a couple
of pickle smoochers.

15:10.701 --> 15:12.268
It's like he ain't
even my son no more,

15:12.303 --> 15:14.103
doing all that
fancy-pants stuff.

15:14.138 --> 15:15.872
Oh, Peter,
I know you miss him,

15:15.906 --> 15:17.674
but this is what
you wanted, isn't it?

15:17.708 --> 15:20.243
Well, yeah, but I didn't
realize bettering himself

15:20.277 --> 15:22.078
meant blowing off
his old man.

15:22.112 --> 15:23.713
You know what?
The hell with this.

15:23.747 --> 15:26.049
I want my son back.
Peter, relax.

15:26.083 --> 15:27.951
Chris is just going
through a phase.

15:27.985 --> 15:29.586
He'll grow out of it.

15:29.620 --> 15:33.356
Just like you grew out of your
"Walk Like An Egyptian" phase.

15:33.390 --> 15:35.725
Paul was a good man.

15:35.759 --> 15:37.627
A good friend.

15:37.661 --> 15:42.932
I'll... I'll always remember
how supportive he was when...

15:42.967 --> 15:44.934
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

15:44.969 --> 15:46.669
I can't-I can't-
I can't do this.

15:55.079 --> 15:56.646
Check it out, Lois--
when Chris notices

15:56.680 --> 15:58.615
that "World's Greatest
Son" mug I made,

15:58.649 --> 16:01.251
he'll be back on board with
his old man in no time.

16:01.285 --> 16:03.052
Hey, Chris,
what's on the mug?

16:03.087 --> 16:05.421
"World's Greatest Sun."

16:05.456 --> 16:07.724
This world only has one sun.

16:07.758 --> 16:10.727
What kind of idiot
made this mug?

16:13.197 --> 16:16.165
♪ When everything's
made to be broken ♪

16:17.468 --> 16:22.672
♪ I just want you
to know who I am. ♪

16:25.209 --> 16:27.977
♪ It might seems crazy
what I'm about to say... ♪

16:30.381 --> 16:33.149
I'm very easily
influenced by music.

16:35.486 --> 16:38.021
All right, Chris, now I know
you're all sophisticated now,

16:38.055 --> 16:40.957
but no man can resist
the siren call

16:40.991 --> 16:43.760
of mentally impaired
supermodel Kate Upton.

16:43.794 --> 16:45.295
Mm, sorry, Dad.

16:45.329 --> 16:47.764
I'm more of a Catherine
Keener man now.

16:47.798 --> 16:49.365
But, Chris, you got
to take advantage.

16:49.400 --> 16:51.000
The clock's ticking
on Kate Upton.

16:51.035 --> 16:52.402
She's almost at the end
of her window.

16:53.537 --> 16:55.038
Yup, we missed it.

16:55.072 --> 16:56.606
Come on, get out of here.

17:04.582 --> 17:06.382
Sorry, Lois, I need the TV.

17:06.417 --> 17:07.951
I gotta win my son back.

17:07.985 --> 17:09.485
What are you
talking about, Peter?

17:09.520 --> 17:10.787
Well, you know how
Brian's got Chris

17:10.821 --> 17:12.956
watching all them fancy
foreign films now?

17:12.990 --> 17:14.657
Well, I figure I can
lure Chris in

17:14.692 --> 17:17.393
<i>with this French movie--
Le Rocqu├й Trois.</i>

17:21.799 --> 17:23.399
<i>Rocqu├й!</i>

17:23.434 --> 17:24.601
<i>Adrienne!</i>

17:24.635 --> 17:26.536
<i>Rocqu├й!
Adrienne!</i>

17:26.570 --> 17:28.638
<i>J'pit├й Le fool.</i>

17:28.672 --> 17:30.907
<i>Oh, Clubbert Lange!</i>

17:30.941 --> 17:32.609
Peter, this
is pointless.

17:32.643 --> 17:34.677
You wanted Chris
to be a better person

17:34.712 --> 17:35.945
with a bright future,

17:35.980 --> 17:38.982
and thanks to Brian,
that's what he has now.

17:39.016 --> 17:41.517
Yeah, but, I don't even
recognize Chris these days.

17:41.552 --> 17:43.519
And I never get
to spend time with him.

17:43.554 --> 17:45.822
It's like Chris
ain't my friend no more.

17:53.097 --> 17:55.365
Thanks for coming to the book
club meeting, everyone.

17:55.399 --> 17:57.934
Well, I gotta say,
I loved this week's book--

17:57.968 --> 18:00.036
<i>Annie Proulx's
The Shipping News.</i>

18:00.070 --> 18:01.638
Yes, yes! Yes.

18:01.672 --> 18:03.873
<i>Very Portnoy's Complaint.</i>

18:03.907 --> 18:06.809
Uh, I don't... What, uh,
what do you mean by that?

18:06.844 --> 18:08.945
Oh, you know, just
with... the-the port,

18:08.979 --> 18:10.613
and the shipping.

18:10.648 --> 18:12.749
<i>And of course,
in Portnoy's Complaint</i>

18:12.783 --> 18:14.917
you've got the guy who's
living near the water...

18:14.952 --> 18:16.853
That doesn't sound right.

18:16.887 --> 18:19.622
...and he hated the noise
from the local port,

18:19.657 --> 18:21.324
so he filed one of
the very first

18:21.358 --> 18:24.193
port noise complaints
in maritime law.

18:24.228 --> 18:25.962
And for those of us who
read the complaints,

18:25.996 --> 18:28.097
trust me, they were
very warranted.

18:28.132 --> 18:30.166
Brian, could I talk
to you over here?

18:30.200 --> 18:31.968
Oh, let's dog-ear this
for a sec.

18:33.303 --> 18:35.538
This one gets it.

18:35.572 --> 18:37.607
Brian, what the hell
are you talking about?

18:37.641 --> 18:39.575
You clearly haven't read
either of those books.

18:39.610 --> 18:42.078
Whoa, Chris, now you're starting
to sound like Mark Twain,

18:42.112 --> 18:44.080
whose real name
was Samuel Clemens.

18:44.114 --> 18:45.848
Oh, that is so your go-to!

18:45.883 --> 18:47.517
I can't believe you.

18:47.551 --> 18:50.219
I thought you were this
worldly, sophisticated guy,

18:50.254 --> 18:53.322
but you're just a fraud--
like Moses.

18:53.357 --> 18:55.358
And the Lord's Eleventh
Commandment--

18:55.392 --> 18:58.327
"Moses gets to be
first in line at the buffet."

18:58.362 --> 19:00.296
What?!
Oh, seriously?

19:00.330 --> 19:02.131
I know, you guys,
it's so lame,

19:02.166 --> 19:04.067
but that's what it says.

19:14.912 --> 19:16.279
Why are you doing that?

19:16.313 --> 19:18.614
Why are you hucking
tater tots at a baby panda?

19:18.649 --> 19:20.349
'Cause I'm oddly
attracted to him

19:20.384 --> 19:21.517
and it frightens me.

19:21.552 --> 19:23.619
See, this is why
you're so great, Dad.

19:23.654 --> 19:25.888
You don't pretend
to be someone you're not.

19:25.923 --> 19:29.325
You do what you want, and you
don't care what anyone thinks.

19:29.359 --> 19:31.194
Well, I-I care what you think.

19:31.228 --> 19:34.831
Look, Chris, you're my kid,
and I want what's best for you,

19:34.865 --> 19:37.100
but I guess
I just don't want you

19:37.134 --> 19:39.102
to become too good
for your old man.

19:39.136 --> 19:40.236
Don't worry, Dad.

19:40.270 --> 19:41.738
I could never be
too good for you

19:41.772 --> 19:43.139
because you're the best.

19:43.173 --> 19:45.775
And I want to be just like you
when I grow up.

19:45.809 --> 19:49.178
Really?
Aw, thanks, Chris.

19:49.213 --> 19:50.446
Tater tot?

19:53.283 --> 19:54.851
It's fun, right?
Yeah!

19:54.885 --> 19:57.386
And the mama panda doesn't
do anything about it,

19:57.421 --> 20:00.356
because she was blinded to make
her more submissive.

20:00.390 --> 20:01.557
Ha-ha! You're right.

20:01.592 --> 20:03.459
You know, Chris,
all this started

20:03.494 --> 20:06.129
'cause I was feeling bad
about being a failure.

20:06.163 --> 20:08.364
But you know what?
I got you for a son.

20:08.398 --> 20:11.134
So maybe I did something right.

20:11.168 --> 20:13.102
I love you, Dad.

20:16.874 --> 20:18.174
Come on, Chris.

20:18.208 --> 20:21.244
Let's go get an $18 Coke at
the terrible zoo restaurant.

20:21.278 --> 20:25.047
Yay! Sticky tables
covered in bees!

20:29.653 --> 20:31.187
Coming up, we'll tell you
where to look

20:31.221 --> 20:33.422
when talking to a coworker
in the bathroom.

20:33.457 --> 20:35.291
The answer
might surprise you.

20:35.325 --> 20:37.915
Well, I think we all
learned something today.

20:37.916 --> 20:40.017
In other news,
TVs still make noise,

20:40.051 --> 20:41.885
even when people
resume their conversations.

20:41.920 --> 20:43.320
Yes, studies have determined...
That's right, Peter.

20:43.655 --> 20:45.255
And it's the most important lesson.

20:45.290 --> 20:46.530
Everyone should be
who they are.

20:47.341 --> 20:48.661
Success is being
true to yourself.

20:49.538 --> 20:50.938
Amen, Mom.

20:50.973 --> 20:53.886
But to have the love of your
family, that's true success.

20:54.049 --> 20:55.216
Let's go ahead and
turn off the TV.

20:55.250 --> 20:57.218
There we go.

20:57.252 --> 21:03.752
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru